% === provenance: Landman2016Empirical ===
% CLAIM: The claim that program-complexity measurement adds nothing to
%   measuring program size does not hold up on large modern corpora.
% FOUND-VIA: scholar search '("cyclomatic complexity" OR "software
%   complexity metric") AND ("lines of code" OR LOC) AND (correlation OR
%   redundant OR criticism OR critique)' -n variables-kiss (Q7) on dblp
%   ('cyclomatic complexity'), ieee, openalex, wos and scopus; the
%   journal version's DOI 10.1002/smr.1760 confirmed against Crossref.
% PICKED: the largest and most recent test of the proxy-for-size claim,
%   and cited specifically because it cuts AGAINST the source it
%   answers, [[Shepperd1988Critique]].  Including it is what makes this
%   a two-sided review rather than a selection of congenial critiques.
%   The 2016 journal article is preferred to the 2014 ICSME conference
%   paper (10.1109/ICSME.2014.44) because it adds the C corpus.
% QUOTE (Abstract): "Our conclusion is that the observed linear
%   correlation between CC and SLOC of Java methods or C functions is not
%   strong enough to conclude that CC is redundant with SLOC.  This
%   conclusion contradicts earlier claims from literature, but concurs
%   with the widely accepted practice of measuring of CC next to SLOC."
%   (Sec. 5.1): "There is no evidence for strong linear correlation
%   between CC and SLOC.  Lower R2 values can be attributed to high
%   variance of CC for the whole range of SLOC."  (Sec. 1): "we will
%   conclude that CC cannot be discarded based on experimental evidence
%   of a linear correlation."
% VERIFIED: full text of the authors' preprint read
%   (https://www.win.tue.nl/~aserebre/Landman2015-ccsloc-jsep2015-preprint.pdf);
%   quotes located by section because the preprint is not paginated to
%   the journal.  Currency checked: a corrigendum exists,
%   doi 10.1002/smr.1914, J. Softw. Evol. Process 29(10):e1914 (2017),
%   read via https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/27002; it corrects plotting bugs,
%   R2 values off by at most 0.01, and axis labelling, and states that
%   "None of the issues reported in this erratum influence the
%   conclusions of the original paper."  scholar verify over the whole
%   session reported this paper as neither retracted nor superseded.
%   NOTE FOR ANYONE REUSING THIS ENTRY: the title reads like more
%   anti-metric evidence and the paper argues the opposite.  Citing it
%   from its title would invert its meaning.
% COUNTER: this entry IS the counter-search result for
%   [[Shepperd1988Critique]].  Its own limitation, stated by the authors,
%   is that it addresses only whether the metric is redundant with size,
%   not whether the metric measures anything a developer cares about --
%   that question is answered negatively by
%   [[Lavazza2023Understandability]].
% DATE: 2026-09-03
@article{Landman2016Empirical,
  author  = {Landman, Davy and Serebrenik, Alexander and Bouwers, Eric and Vinju, Jurgen J.},
  title   = {Empirical Analysis of the Relationship between {CC} and {SLOC} in a Large Corpus of {Java} Methods and {C} Functions},
  journal = {Journal of Software: Evolution and Process},
  year    = {2016},
  volume  = {28},
  number  = {7},
  pages   = {589--618},
  doi     = {10.1002/smr.1760}
}

% === provenance: MunozBaron2020Cognitive ===
% CLAIM: At least one measure of code complexity does track how hard
%   developers actually find code to understand.
% FOUND-VIA: scholar search '(understandability OR comprehension) AND
%   ("cognitive complexity" OR "code complexity") AND (predict OR
%   correlation OR validation)' -n variables-kiss (Q15) on dblp
%   ('cognitive complexity understandability'), openalex, wos and scopus
%   (ieee was quota-exhausted by then and is recorded as provider-down
%   for Q15); DOI 10.1145/3382494.3410636 confirmed against Crossref.
% PICKED: the strongest positive empirical evidence found for C2 --
%   a meta-analysis over existing comprehension studies rather than a
%   single experiment, so it is the best available answer to "does any
%   of this show up in real developers?".  Cited together with its
%   opposite number [[Lavazza2023Understandability]].
% QUOTE (Sec. 5.1, Findings): "With respect to the results of our
%   meta-analysis, we found that Cognitive Complexity correlates with the
%   time taken to comprehend code snippets (0.54) and with the perceived
%   understandability ratings from developers (-0.29)."  (Sec. 3, RQ1):
%   "Does Cognitive Complexity correlate with measures of source code
%   understandability from existing studies?"
% VERIFIED: full text read from the authors' arXiv copy
%   (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2007.12520); quotes located by section, since
%   the preprint's pagination is its own and not the proceedings'.
% COUNTER: the counter-search is the same query Q15 on the same
%   providers, and it returned the opposing result, which is cited:
%   [[Lavazza2023Understandability]] finds code measures explain
%   understandability only partially.  Note also the effect sizes here
%   are moderate at best (0.54 and -0.29), so this source supports C2
